ord.message  # no, would modify shared record msg\n```\nBetter not modify record.msg. Instead, temporarily: save original msg/args, set record.msg = truncated, record.args = None, call super().format, then restore in finally. That's messy but the requirement says \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args\" — must not rewrite shared record's attributes; restoring is fine but risky.\n\nCleaner: implement formatting ourselves partially? Use `super().format` but override record.message after? The default Formatter.format does:\n\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): record.asctime = ...\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\nif record.exc_info: ... s += '\\n' + self.formatException\nif record.stack_info: s += '\\n' + self.formatStack\n```\n\nSo a clean approach: compute message, if truncated, use a copy of the record. We can copy the record? Copy would be safe: `import copy`; rec = copy.copy(record); rec.message = truncated; rec.msg = truncated; rec.args = None. Careful with exc_info — for exception traceback formatting, `formatException` uses record.exc_info, fine.\n\nBut copying has an issue: record.args is a Mapping etc. Setting rec.args = () and rec.msg = truncated is fine for the copy.\n\nNote that `formatException` may use `record.exc_text` caching — copy is per-call so no caching across records; fine, though exception text computed each time (small perf). Acceptable.\n\nActually simpler alternative: use a temporary subclass approach:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if need truncation:\n        record = copy.copy(record) ... \n```\nLet's do that. But careful: the multi-line prefix logic uses `record.message`, so we set both record.message and record.msg on the copy.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\ndef _truncate_message(msg: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    marker = _OMIT_MARKER  # \"...<omitted>...\"\n    if len(msg) <= budget:\n        return msg\n    keep = budget - len(marker)\n    head = (keep + 1) // 2\n    tail = keep - head\n    return msg[:head] + marker + (msg[len(msg) - tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\nCheck budget=32: marker len 15. keep=17, head=9, tail=8. matches example: 开头9, 末尾8, marker 15 => total 32. Good. But note if len(msg) > budget but budget-marker small... budget>=32 so keep>=17 fine.\n\nAlso note: after truncation, len <= budget. If tail is 0, no tail part.\n\nFor the copy in format, do:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n        if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > max_chars:\n                record = self._with_truncated_message(record, message, max_chars)\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\nNote: after copying, `record.message` will be recomputed by super().format from msg/args. So on copy set msg=truncated, args=().\n\n```python\n    def _shrink(self, record, message, max_chars):\n        shrunk = copy.copy(record)\n        shrunk.msg = _truncate_message(message, max_chars)\n        shrunk.args = None\n        return shrunk\n```\nBut careful: copying a LogRecord — copy.copy works on instances (has __dict__). LogRecord defines __dict__ so fine. Also exc_info is kept by reference.\n\nHmm, potential issue: exception formatting uses `record.exc_text` attribute; LogRecord may not have exc_text attribute set initially? It sets `self.exc_text = None` in __init__ I believe. Fine.\n\nAnother consideration: `getMessage()` on copy not called since super().format calls record.getMessage() which does `if self.args: msg = msg % self.args`. With args=None → returns msg. Good.\n\nAlso for the copy, need `record.message` — super().format sets it.\n\nOne more concern: the multi-line prefix code: `prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]` — record.message is the truncated message from super().format on the copy. Fine.\n\nAlso keep_full_message check: `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True`.\n\nDefault init: `_setup_logger` passes `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Import envs in logger.py. Check for circular import: environ.py imports only stdlib, so ok. But logger.py importing environ at module level... environ doesn't import logger. Fine.\n\nBut careful: `_setup_logger` is called at import. Accessing envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS triggers env var read at import — could raise ValueError at import if invalid; acceptable per spec.\n\nRequirement: \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — existing code already handles via `_default_handler is None`. But note the formatter is recreated each `_setup_logger` call, which is what we want (re-init applies new config value). Does anything call _setup_logger again? Only init_logger? No — init_logger doesn't call it. Tests may call `_setup_logger()` again. Fine.\n\nAlso need: the formatter for the default handler must use config; existing old formatters keep old budget. Fine.\n\nLet me also check the logger.py lines 33-96 region and check the constructor signature validation:\n\n```python\n        if max_message_chars is not None and (\n            isinstance(max_message_chars, bool)\n            or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int)\n            or max_message_chars < 32\n        ):\n            raise ValueError(...)\n```\n\nNow stats_logger: change `logger.info(m) ` to `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nenviron.py: add helper and descriptor. Where to add? In `# -- Logging --` section.\n\n```python\ndef _log_max_message_chars(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    limit = int(v)\n    if limit < 32:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be an integer >= 32\")\n    return limit\n```\nEmpty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError(\"invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\") — message must contain variable name; the descriptor wraps with name prefix. Good.\n\nNow write edits. Let's check baseline already done (baseline_done:1). Phase edit